                <pb facs="0540" n="528" sameAs="frus:frus1943v05/pg528"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">810.74/990: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Argentina</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Buenos
                                Aires</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1943-07-03T16:00:00-03:00">July 3, 1943—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 4:18 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1481. Your 955, June 29, 3 p.m. Both Hayes and the British feel that only
                    solution is to have Radiobras refuse to transmit coded messages to neutral
                    countries.</p>
                <p>Hayes informs that the Japanese are sending messages in code to Lisbon via
                    British Western.</p>
                <p>As Italcable is still functioning between Buenos Aires and Brazil, it will be
                    very difficult to check on traffic. All America Cables and British Western have
                    been asked to furnish information requested.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Armour</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
